                    <div data-drupal-messages-fallback class="hidden"></div><div id="block-gao-uswds-content" class="block block-system block-system-main-block" data-entity-id="system_main_block" data-entity-type="block">
  
    
      


<article class="node node--type-blog node--view-mode-full">
<div class="node_content">
<div class="two-col-wrapper">
	<div class="layout layout--twocol-section layout--twocol-section--75-25">

					<div  class="layout__region layout__region--first">
				<div class="block block-layout-builder block-field-blocknodeblogbody" data-entity-id="field_block:node:blog:body" data-entity-type="block">
  
    
      
            <div class="field field--name-body field--type-text-with-summary field--label-hidden field__item"><div><p>After disaster strikes, people’s lives can be turned upside down. It can be difficult to recover and rebuild. People with disabilities may face additional hardships trying to get help after disasters.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p>By law, the federal government must incorporate the needs of people with disabilities into disaster planning and response. But is it doing enough?&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p>Today’s WatchBlog post looks at <a href="/products/gao-26-108057" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="7484b853-a136-4561-8241-ba53537d0656" data-entity-substitution="canonical" title="Disaster Assistance: FEMA Actions Needed to Better Support People with Disabilities in Disasters">our new report</a> on this issue.</p><figure class="media media--view-mode-default media--type-image">
  
      
  <div class="field field--name-field-media-image field--type-image field--label-visually_hidden">
    <h2 class="field__label visually-hidden">Image</h2>
              <div class="field__item">  <img loading="lazy" src="/assets/styles/default/public/2026-11/Wheelchair%20graphic_0.png.webp?itok=5fcefWxD" width="550" height="400" alt="Photo of a wheelchair in a flooded area">


</div>
          </div>

  </figure>
<h2>The government steps in during times of crisis</h2><div><p>When a major disaster hits, people with disabilities often face the toughest challenges recovering. To support them and other survivors, the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) teams up with state and local partners, such as governments and nonprofits.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p>Support for people with disabilities can take many forms, but generally falls into the categories of providing information, specialized staff, and financial assistance.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p><strong>Information. </strong>FEMA has developed some guidance for state and local partners on how to incorporate the needs of people with disabilities into disaster planning and response. For example, FEMA’s guidance for emergency shelters outlines considerations for service animals and recommendations for stocking things like hearing aids or screen readers. Also, people with intellectual or developmental disabilities may need a quiet space or noise-canceling headphones to cope in a crowded shelter. And people who use wheelchairs need ramps and accessible bathrooms.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p><strong>Training.</strong> In the past, FEMA has provided on-demand training for state and local emergency response agencies focused on helping people with disabilities in disaster response. One FEMA official told us demand for the course was consistently high.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p><strong>Specialized staff.</strong> Each FEMA region has a disability specialist who is tasked with working with state and local governments to help include the needs of people with disabilities in disaster planning and response.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p><strong>Financial assistance.</strong> FEMA also offers grants to help disaster survivors recover. This can include assistance with temporary housing, medical expenses, and money to rebuild or repair homes. For people with disabilities, this can also include replacement of medical equipment like wheelchairs. It may also cover the addition of ramps and grab bars to their house—even if those things were not in place before the disaster.&nbsp;</p><h2>FEMA assistance is available, but is it accessible?</h2><div><p>Yes…and no. Recent changes along with previously existing issues at FEMA have impacted assistance and supports for people with disabilities. For example, FEMA no longer trains its partners on how to include people with disabilities in disaster response. In 2025, the agency removed the training on how to do this from its curriculum to comply with the administration’s new Executive Orders. And concerns are mounting that without this training, state and local partners risk not being able to help people with disabilities.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p>In addition, coordination with partners before disasters has been inconsistent. In some regions, disability specialists proactively work with state and local partners to ensure that the needs of people with disabilities are considered. This includes planning for evacuation of people with disabilities. But in other regions, this isn’t happening. Local partners in two of three states we looked at told us they have not coordinated with the disability specialist for their region before a disaster. Some even said they were not aware of who the disability specialist for their region was. Agency downsizing and staff turnover among disability specialists may be partly to blame.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p>Finally, some people with disabilities continue to face obstacles when applying for disaster money through FEMA. For example, the application for disaster money has questions that may use difficult words without explaining them, and the letter that tells applicants if they will receive money can be hard for some people with disabilities to understand. And while FEMA has revised its letter to use more plain language and simplified its appeals process, the application process may still be difficult for some.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p>Getting feedback from people with different types of disabilities on the application process and making related adjustments could help FEMA ensure that those who are particularly vulnerable in the aftermath of a disaster receive the help they are eligible for.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p><a href="/products/gao-26-108057" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="7484b853-a136-4561-8241-ba53537d0656" data-entity-substitution="canonical" title="Disaster Assistance: FEMA Actions Needed to Better Support People with Disabilities in Disasters">Our full report</a> outlines FEMA’s role and steps it can take to better support people with disabilities, including reinstituting and improving partner training, coordinating with partner agencies before disasters happen, and improving the application process for assistance. We are also issuing an easy-read version of our report soon to be accessible to people with certain disabilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><hr></div><div><ul><li>GAO’s fact-based, nonpartisan information helps Congress and federal agencies improve government. The WatchBlog lets us contextualize GAO’s work a little more for the public. Check out more of our posts at <a href="/blog" data-entity-type="node" data-entity-uuid="1909c989-899a-489c-8abf-f1ac8626cddf" data-entity-substitution="canonical" title="WatchBlog">GAO.gov/blog</a>. &nbsp;</li></ul></div><div><ul><li>Got a comment, question? Email us at blog@gao.gov&nbsp;</li></ul></div></div></div></div>
      
  </div>
